"Aristotle also asked which stories do the work. A thoroughly good man brought to ruin, he says, moves neither pity nor fear; it merely shocks us. A bad man who prospers satisfies nothing. The fall of an utter villain pleases our moral sense and is not tragic, for pity is aroused by unmerited misfortune, fear by the misfortune of a man like ourselves. What remains is someone between the extremes, a person better than most of us and not perfect, whose ruin comes not by vice or depravity, but by some error or frailty. The word he uses, hamartia, means missing the mark. Oedipus is his example: a capable and well-meaning king who does not know who his parents are.": "ecd0eee63115",
"Aristotle answered his teacher in a single clause. Tragedy, he wrote, is the imitation of a serious action, through pity and fear effecting the proper purgation of these emotions. The Greek word is katharsis, and the Poetics never explains it. On the oldest reading it is medical: the play draws off an excess of pity and fear and leaves us lighter, so that Plato had the effect backward. On another it means clarification: by feeling pity and fear toward the right objects in the right way, we come to understand those feelings better. Aristotle adds a second point that needs no interpretation. We enjoy accurate likenesses even of things that are painful to see, such as the forms of the most ignoble animals and of dead bodies, because to learn gives the liveliest pleasure. A tragedy shows how a certain kind of person comes to a certain kind of end, and seeing how things happen is a pleasure in itself.": "aa22ffaaae8f",
"As a student in Carthage, Augustine loved the theater, and years later he could not understand his younger self. Why is it, he asked, that man desires to be made sad, beholding doleful and tragical things, which yet himself would by no means suffer? yet he desires as a spectator to feel sorrow at them, and this very sorrow is his pleasure. Hume began an essay on the subject in the same puzzled tone: It seems an unaccountable pleasure, which the spectators of a well-written tragedy receive from sorrow, terror, anxiety, and other passions, that are in themselves disagreeable and uneasy. The more the audience is made to weep, he observed, the better pleased it is.": "61e8132e9e92",
"Audiences come away saying that they enjoyed themselves.": "9cceef626cb4",
"Audiences reject stories that pile on misery without shape.": "9413537b4624",
"Confucius set a measure that Aristotle would have recognized. Of the first poem in the Book of Songs he said that it is joyful without being wanton, and sorrowful without doing injury. A good sad poem lets grief be felt fully and leaves the reader whole. This is a claim about art and about character at once, since it supposes that feeling can be given a form that the person is able to carry.": "5ae66d1aafc1",
"His question travels well, and his answers less well.": "5548ce9ba1af",
"Hume proposed that the pain is real and is transformed. A tragedy gives two things at once: a painful subject, and the delight of eloquent language, vivid imagination, and skilled imitation. When the second is the stronger, it takes over the energy of the first, so that the whole impulse of those passions is converted into pleasure. Bad writing on the same subject would only distress us. The theory explains why craft matters. It has less to say about why a painful subject should be better than a cheerful one.": "3b0130d3cfd3",
"In our own misfortunes we hold the impulse in check.": "bc7e6ae45b0b",
"It seems an unaccountable pleasure.": "725a2d076c00",
"Modern philosophers have pressed a prior question. We know that Anna Karenina never lived. It seems that to pity someone one must believe that she exists and suffers. And yet readers weep for her. The three statements cannot all be true together. Colin Radford concluded in 1975 that our response to fiction is simply irrational. Kendall Walton argued that what we feel is not quite pity. We take part in a game of make-believe, and within the game it is true that we grieve, as a child playing at monsters is afraid and does not run from the room. A third answer gives up the claim that emotion requires belief. Vivid thoughts are enough to move us, which is why we can be frightened by imagining an accident that has not happened.": "2e24d9d2b0ca",
"Nietzsche made the largest claim of all. The Greeks, he wrote in his first book, knew the terrors of existence. Their folk wisdom said that the best thing is not to be born and the next best to die soon. Tragedy was how they faced this and went on living. In the theater the worst was shown, with music and in beautiful form, to a whole city sitting together, and the spectators went home stronger. The pleasure of tragedy, on his account, is the pleasure of finding that one can look at the truth and still say yes to life.": "295fded7844a",
"On this reading Plato had the effect backward.": "6097f54a4203",
"Plato thought the pleasure a weakness. In our own misfortunes, he says, we feel a natural hunger and desire to relieve our sorrow by weeping and lamentation, and we hold it in check. At the theater we let it loose, since the sorrow is anothers and there seems to be no shame in it. But the habit grows. Poetry feeds and waters the passions instead of drying them up, and a person who has wept freely over a stage hero will find it harder to bear his own troubles calmly. For this reason Plato kept the tragic poets out of his ideal city.": "7636aaeec53f",
"She is framed for murder and beheaded.": "f89df8c619e8",
"Sorrow is a state we go to great trouble to avoid.": "f4fa0b4d13df",
"The Buddha used sad stories and was exact about what they are for. In one short discourse he asks the monks which is greater, the water in the four oceans or the tears they have shed in the long course of their wandering, weeping and wailing from being coupled with the unloved and separated from the loved. The tears are more, he says. He goes through the losses one by one, a mother, a father, a child, and ends: This is quite enough for you to become disillusioned, dispassionate, and freed regarding all conditions. The image is a tragedy on the largest possible scale, and it aims at neither relief nor enjoyment. The feeling it is meant to arouse is called saṃvega, the shock of seeing clearly, which turns a person toward the path. Catharsis, on the medical reading, sends the spectator home lighter. Saṃvega is meant to make him change direction.": "6c8620cd5d68",
"The commentaries tell the story of Kisā Gotamī, who carried her dead child from house to house asking for medicine. The Buddha told her to bring a mustard seed from a home where no one had died. She found none, and understood. The story works as Aristotle says a tragedy should, by recognition: a private grief is seen to be the common lot. Buddhist teaching adds a distinction that answers Augustines worry about the spectator. Grief over a loss is suffering and nothing more. Compassion is the wish that suffering end, and it is steady where grief is not. A sad story is well used when it leaves a person with more of the second and less need of the first, which is close to what Confucius meant by sorrow that does no injury (Dharma and Western Philosophy D19).": "7e2295d62767",
"The great books were the work of sages giving vent to their indignation.": "1e56d2e36cda",
"The greatest tragedy of the Chinese stage breaks this rule. In Guan Hanqings The Injustice to Dou E, written in the thirteenth century, a young widow who has done nothing wrong is framed for murder and beheaded. Before she dies she calls on Heaven for three signs, among them snow in midsummer, and they come. Aristotle would say that the play merely shocks. Chinese audiences found in it what they wanted from a sad story, which was a protest. Confucius had said that poetry can be used to express grievance, and the historian Sima Qian, after his own unjust punishment, wrote that the great books were mostly the work of sages and worthies giving vent to their indignation. On this view a sad story exists to keep a wrong from being forgotten.": "8e041872605d",
"The puzzle is real. Sorrow and fear are states we go to great trouble to avoid. A sad story produces them on purpose, and we seek it out and recommend it to our friends. Either the feelings we have at a tragedy are not really sorrow and fear, or they are, and something else is going on that makes them worth having.": "0b0c0638d7a8",
"The stronger feeling takes over the energy of the weaker.": "c674255f0118",
"Tragedy, then, is an imitation of an action that is serious, complete, and of a certain magnitude; in language embellished with each kind of artistic ornament, the several kinds being found in separate parts of the play; in the form of action, not of narrative; through pity and fear effecting the proper purgation of these emotions. [] There remains, then, the character between these two extremes,—that of a man who is not eminently good and just, yet whose misfortune is brought about not by vice or depravity, but by some error or frailty.": "7e3e16147583",
"Why is it, that man desires to be made sad, beholding doleful and tragical things, which yet himself would by no means suffer? yet he desires as a spectator to feel sorrow at them, and this very sorrow is his pleasure. What is this but a miserable madness? for a man is the more affected with these actions, the less free he is from such affections.": "26deec23f904",
